<interrogationes unitas="flatura">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I gzip a buffer and get it back?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_gzip_deflare flatura_gzip_inflare flatura_status_nuntium">flatura_gzip_deflare(datum,
      mensura, FLATURA_COMPRESSIO_ORDINARIA, piscina) and
      flatura_gzip_inflare(datum, mensura, piscina). Both return a
      FlaturaFructus by value: check status == FLATURA_STATUS_OK before
      using datum and mensura; on failure datum is NIHIL and
      flatura_status_nuntium names the status. The output is allocated
      in the piscina you pass and lives as long as it does. Output bytes
      are binary, not a NUL-terminated string.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I inflate zlib data (PNG, git objects) rather than gzip?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_inflare flatura_deflare" via="lib/git.c lib/imago_png.c">There
      is no zlib wrapper here. Check and skip the 2-byte zlib header
      yourself (a preset dictionary, FDICT, cannot be handled), call
      flatura_inflare on the raw DEFLATE data, and check the Adler-32 in
      the last 4 bytes yourself: _zlib_inflare in lib/git.c does all
      three, and lib/imago_png.c is the writing side (flatura_deflare
      plus header and Adler-32). flatura_inflare stops at the final
      block and ignores bytes after it, but it does not tell you how
      many input bytes it consumed, so the Adler-32 can only be found
      when the stream ends exactly at the end of your buffer.
      Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): on zlib data from another
      encoder where a stored block follows a dynamic-Huffman block,
      flatura_inflare can drop a byte of the stored block's LEN/NLEN
      and fail a valid PNG or git object with FLATURA_STATUS_FRACTA_DATUM
      (see the untrusted-sources answer below).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I stream data through flatura, or tell truncated input from corrupt input?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_inflare flatura_gzip_inflare">No.
      Each call takes the whole input and produces the whole output in
      one go. FLATURA_STATUS_INCOMPLETUS and FLATURA_STATUS_FRACTA_ALLOCATIO
      exist in the enum but are never returned (only
      flatura_status_nuntium names them): input that ends early comes
      back as an ordinary error, usually FLATURA_STATUS_FRACTA_DATUM
      (FLATURA_STATUS_FRACTA_DISTANTIA if it ends inside a distance
      code), exactly like garbage, and an allocation failure exits the
      process inside piscina_allocare. lib/git.c waits for INCOMPLETUS
      to grow its pack window, so that growth never happens.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I limit how much a decompression may produce?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_inflare piscina_allocare piscina_generare_certae_magnitudinis">No:
      there is no output limit parameter. The output buffer starts at IV
      * MXXIV bytes and doubles, each old buffer staying in the arena,
      so a decompression bomb grows the piscina until piscina_allocare
      gives up and exits the process. A piscina from
      piscina_generare_certae_magnitudinis caps it, but the process
      still exits instead of getting an error.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does flatura compress worse than gzip -9 even at level 9?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_deflare">flatura_deflare
      always writes a single block with the FIXED Huffman code (no
      dynamic Huffman tables). The level only changes how far the LZ77
      search follows the hash chain: VIII candidates for level 1,
      CXXVIII for levels 2 to 8, IV * MXXIV for 9 and up.
      FLATURA_COMPRESSIO_NULLA writes stored blocks of at most 65535
      bytes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I size an output buffer with flatura_maxima_magnitudo_compressa?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_maxima_magnitudo_compressa flatura_deflare">Only
      for FLATURA_COMPRESSIO_NULLA. The bound assumes stored blocks (5
      bytes per 65535 plus 18 for gzip framing), but the compressing
      levels use the fixed Huffman code, where bytes 144 to 255 cost 9
      bits each, so incompressible input can exceed the bound by up to
      about an eighth of its size (1000 such bytes gzip to 1145 against
      a bound of 1023). Even with NULLA, empty input breaks it: the
      bound for 0 bytes is 18, but the empty stored block makes a
      23-byte gzip file. You never need it for flatura itself, which
      allocates its own output.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute a CRC-32 over data that arrives in pieces?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_crc32_continuare flatura_crc32" via="lib/imago_png.c">Start
      with 0xFFFFFFFF, pass it through flatura_crc32_continuare for each
      piece, and XOR the final value with 0xFFFFFFFF. flatura_crc32 does
      all three for one buffer, so its result is already finalized: do
      not feed it back into flatura_crc32_continuare. It is the IEEE
      802.3 CRC used by gzip and PNG.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can flatura_gzip_inflare read multi-member gzip files or files with trailing bytes?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_gzip_inflare">No.
      It reads the CRC-32 and size from the LAST 8 bytes of the buffer
      and inflates only the first member, so a second member or any
      padding after the trailer makes the check fail with
      FLATURA_STATUS_GZIP_FRACTA. The header's optional name, comment
      and extra fields are skipped; the header CRC (FHCRC) is skipped,
      not checked. Input shorter than 18 bytes is refused.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I trust flatura_inflare on deflate data from other encoders or from untrusted sources?
    <responsum symbolum="flatura_inflare">Only
      with care. A back-reference distance is not checked against the
      bytes produced so far, so corrupt input that points before the
      start of the output still returns FLATURA_STATUS_OK with bytes
      from the unwritten window instead of FLATURA_STATUS_FRACTA_DISTANTIA.
      The extra-bit reads of the 16/17/18 repeat codes in a dynamic
      header are not checked for end of input. And a stored block
      discards the whole bit buffer to reach a byte boundary, which is
      only right when fewer than 8 bits are buffered. After a fixed
      block that always holds; after a dynamic-Huffman block whose
      end-of-block code is at least 4 bits shorter than its longest
      literal/length code, a whole byte of LEN/NLEN is dropped and a
      VALID stream fails with FLATURA_STATUS_FRACTA_DATUM. zlib rarely
      emits that shape (its end-of-block code is usually among the
      longest), but a hand-built stream that zlib itself decodes
      fails. flatura's own output never mixes the two (one fixed
      block, or only stored blocks).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
